A||N||6||stimpy||Rain tires||||14||
Z||000000||stimpy||10-29-2000||10:50 AM||||Just picked up a new set of treads for my GT and wanted to put out my early endorsment if anyone is looking for new tires and lives in a rainy climate.  Picked up a set of Bridgestone Turanza H Revo (that's a mouthful).  These are classified as Grand Touring tires- meaning they are not as all out performance oriented as say the Bridgestone RE-71 or S-02 Pole Position, but they do make for a very nice ride.  It has been raining like crazy here in Oklahoma lately, and I have aimed at standing water, and puddles don't faze 'em. <P>Any other recommendations out there for people loking to put on new rubber?<BR>||||reg||14
Z||000001||Tom88gt||10-29-2000||10:58 AM||||How about the Dunlop D60 A2 tires.  A favorite with consumer reports.  They call it a touring tire (improved traction but still good rain handling).  Put a set on my, now former, 300ZX and also a pair on my 914.  I've been very happy with them.  When the Kellys wear out I going to the D60s on my Fiero as well.  <P>It's not the ultimate performance tire but still pretty good plus all weather performance.  And you can't beat the price.||||reg||7
Z||000002||Monkeyman||10-29-2000||06:13 PM||||I always thought that touring tires were what was found on big Lincolns and Caddys.  I guess I got a better deal than I thought when the Grape Ape came with brand new Pirelli P4000 Touring tires.  I always wondered why they worked so well both in the rain and when it was dry.  There you go.  That's my recommendation.  (No idea what they cost, though.)||||reg||1
Z||000003||Master_Sushi||10-29-2000||08:21 PM||||I just got 4 Dunlop Dp2 snows put on my stock turbo wheels  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/tongue.gif">. They are pretty nice, obviously not performance oriented. But oddly enough they handel better then the orignal 12 year old ones  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"> . But id have to say a fiero will handel pretty well with just about any tires/wheels on it  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">.||||reg||10
Z||000004||stimpy||10-29-2000||09:49 PM||||There is a difference between touring and grand touring tires, according to the tire guys at least.  Garnd touring puts a little more accent on performance. All-out performance tires look cool, but wear so quickly that for my type of driving it doesn't make sense to go for that type of tire.  Pirelli's are good tires, my dad has P8000's (I think) on his car (and being that he owns a tire shop, I guess he could put pretty much watever he wants on his car).  What sold me on the Bridgestones was the dual tread design. Underneath the surface tread is a softer compound, so as the tires wear, instead of getting hard from the heat as most tires do as they age, the softer compound actually makes a half worn tire stop quicker then a new one.  ||||reg||1
Z||000005||My7Fieros||10-29-2000||09:55 PM||||R r ruh... ruh-a.......ruh-ai.....rrrain? What is that? Oh wait, I think Ive heard of it..its when water falls out of the sky, right?<BR>I wouldnt know..it hasnt rained here in months.....literally.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000006||stimpy||10-29-2000||10:18 PM||||Just a couple of weeks ago the Gov. of Oklahoma was urging his citizens to pray for rain. We hadn't had ANY rainfall for over 100 days.  Well, being the buckle of the Bible belt carries some weight, because in the past two weeks we've had over 10 inches of rain in some parts, along with a couple of tornados thrown in for s&g's.  I miss California osmetimes.||||reg||1
